From 44ad01e9a205ec3066ee208bf51bb9e4a552becc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 06:35:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 289a: should this question be answered at all - and two
 tautologies caught before the run

The first verb that is not a lookup. Four verdicts built from the tape so the
labels are free: ok, wrong_relation, contested, false_premise. The interesting
one is wrong_relation - ask about one relation of a subject while the evidence
answers another - and getting it to be interesting took two corrections, both
made before any compute was spent.

The first draft was a tautology of the same species as 286's exam. Building
"absent" as evidence pulled from other addresses and "false premise" as a
borrowed value makes each class exactly the property a two-line hand rule
reads off; the rule scores about 1.0 and the stage measures nothing. Named in
the docstring rather than quietly fixed, because the species keeps recurring.

The second draft was caught by the offline constructor test, which is why the
test exists. With the question ASSERTING the sibling relation's value, the
counter sees a value it cannot find in the evidence and cries false premise -
so the label was still a property of my construction rather than of the
situation. Fixed by removing the asserted value from ok and wrong_relation
entirely: those two are QUERIES, not claims, and they differ only in the text
of the question. Verified offline that they are now count-identical - same
subject, same evidence, same majority, no tie - and that the rival returns the
same verdict for both. Its blindness is real rather than asserted, exactly as
on 288's duplicated forgery, and its ceiling on a balanced set is about 0.50
by construction, which is now stated in the decision JSON so nobody reads
"beats the rival" as the achievement.

What can separate them is whether the question's own context agrees with the
mentions' - the rank channel the relational mind already has, measured between
the query row and the evidence instead of between two mentions. The query
joins the graph as one more row carrying its own context, and an asserted
value only where the question actually claims one; a query with no claim
matches nobody on the value channel, which is correct, because it is asking.

Output is one of four verdicts, so no value is produced and nothing is
generated. Gates: beat the rival and the majority floor, catch wrong_relation
above the rival's recall on it, and do not buy that by crying wolf - the ok
recall must stay at or above the rival's, which is relative and needs no
threshold.

